WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.508 --> 00:00:01.300
CYNTHIA THOMAS: Hi.

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.258
This is Cynthia Thomas.

00:00:02.258 --> 00:00:05.090
I'm a networking
specialist at Google Cloud.

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.039
Welcome back to "What's
New in Networking."

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:09.960
Today, we're going
to talk about Anthos

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.810
and compute networking
launches, so stay tuned.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.219
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.540
Let's start with Kubernetes.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.120
As more enterprises
adopt Kubernetes,

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.350
new requirements are coming up
around multicloud, security,

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.810
and visiblity.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.540
And new technologies,
like Service Mesh,

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.050
demand more customizations
of the underlying Kubernetes

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:37.070
layer.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:40.670
These requirements all need a
more programmable data plane

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:44.480
that can do Kubernetes-aware
networking without sacrificing

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:45.830
performance.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:49.350
I'm pleased to introduce
to you Dataplane V2.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:54.980
Dataplane V2 is an opinionated
data plane for GKE and Anthos

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.590
clusters, and it's
been optimized

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.540
for Kubernetes networking.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:03.140
Dataplane V2 is
implemented with EBPF--

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.960
Extended Berkeley Packet Filter.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.810
This handles network
packets in the kernel

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.590
by configuring the data plane
using Kubernetes-specific

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.426
metadata.

00:01:14.426 --> 00:01:20.060
Dataplane V2 brings several
advantages to your deployments.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.610
From a security and
operations perspective,

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.110
you have native
Kubernetes network policy

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:32.090
and logging without the need
for a third-party add-on.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.930
Network policy logging lets you
monitor how your policies are

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.640
enforcing traffic.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.100
That way, you can see
allowed and denied

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.820
network connections in
the Cloud Logging Console

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.650
to audit and
troubleshoot policies.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:51.500
Dataplane V2 also lends well
to large environments for scale

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.950
without the need for
a qproxy nor iptables

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.260
for service routing.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.500
Finally, the big
thing Dataplane V2

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.260
brings to your
multifaceted environments

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.560
is consistency across
environment types--

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.820
GKE, Anthos vSphere, and
Anthos on bare metal.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.040
With Dataplane V2,
you can quickly

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.830
identify abnormal
traffic patterns

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:19.290
and spot irregular
network activity.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.370
Finally, let's talk
about Google Virtual NIC,

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.770
or gVNIC for short.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.160
It's a virtual network
interface designed specifically

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.400
for Google Compute Engine.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.120
Previously, VMs that
ran on Compute Engine

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.670
used a virtual network interface
based on the Virtio spec.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.890
For better networking
performance

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.260
and to support
additional features,

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:51.770
Google uses gVNIC as its default
virtual network interface.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.350
GVNIC supports higher
network bandwidths,

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:57.320
such as 50 to 100
gigabits a second.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.860
These can be used for
distributed workloads on VMs

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.440
that have attached GPUs.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.180
If you want to use gVNICs, you
can use it on a supported OS

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.720
or on an unsupported OS by
manually installing the drivers

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:13.850
for Linux or Windows.

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:17.330
And that's our update for
Anthos and compute networking.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.490
Check out the links
below for more details

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.468
on these new features.

00:03:21.468 --> 00:03:23.510
Join us next time for
"What's New in Networking."

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.860
